;;;;
;;;; The instruction semantics were established empirically and the resulting
;;;; algorithm was cross-checked against OpenSSL:
;;;;
;;;;   SHA256MSG1 d,s   d[i] += sigma0(d[i+1]) for i < 3, d[3] += sigma0(s[0])
;;;;   SHA256MSG2 d,s   completes the schedule word using s
;;;;   SHA256RNDS2 k,d,s   d := two SHA-256 rounds, where
;;;;                       d enters holding (f,e,b,a) in lanes 0..3
;;;;                       s enters holding (h,g,d,c) in lanes 0..3
;;;;                       and d leaves holding the new (f,e,b,a)
;;;;
;;;; G(g) holds the schedule words W[4g .. 4g+3].  Given G(0..3) from the
;;;; block, for g = 4..15:
;;;;
;;;;   P    := SHA256MSG1(G(g-4), G(g-3))
;;;;   P    := P + PALIGNR(G(g-1), G(g-2), 4)
;;;;   G(g) := SHA256MSG2(P, G(g-1))
;;;;
;;;; and four rounds are driven from G(g):
;;;;
;;;;   MSG := G(g) + K[4g .. 4g+3]
;;;;   B   := SHA256RNDS2(MSG, B, A)
;;;;   MSG := PSHUFD(MSG, 0x0E)
;;;;   A   := SHA256RNDS2(MSG, A, B)
;;;;
;;;; The four message words live in x3..x6 and are rotated once per group, so
;;;; only xmm0..x7 are needed.  xmm0 must hold the W+K operand because it is
;;;; the implicit register of SHA256RNDS2.
;;;;
;;;; PALIGNR d,s,i computes  d := (d.hi64 : s.lo64)  when i is 8, which is how
;;;; the state is repacked on entry and exit.

